<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Reviews do two jobs for a local business. They help Google decide who to show on the map, and they help a stranger decide whether to call you. Most happy customers will leave one if you ask at the right moment and make it easy. This guide covers how to ask, what to say and what the rules do not allow.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">When to ask</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Ask when the customer is most pleased: straight after the job is finished, the order has arrived or the problem is fixed. A week later the moment has passed. If you see customers in person, ask in person and then send the link so they do not have to search for you.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Make it one tap</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In your Google Business Profile, choose "Ask for reviews" to get a short link that opens the review box directly. Put that link in a text message or email, on your invoices and receipts, and as a QR code on a card or at the till. Every extra step you remove means more reviews.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What to say</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Keep the message short, personal and free of pressure. For example: "Thanks for choosing us today. If you have a minute, a Google review really helps a small business like ours. Here is the link." Do not suggest what to write or which star rating to give.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-inline-cta"} -->
