<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What the rules do not allow</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Paying for reviews or offering rewards.</strong> No discounts, gifts or prize draws in return for a review.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Review gating.</strong> Asking only the customers you expect to be positive, or sending unhappy ones somewhere else first.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Writing your own.</strong> That includes staff, family and friends posing as customers.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Buying reviews.</strong> From anyone, anywhere.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Hiding the bad ones.</strong> If you show reviews on your own website, you should not publish only the favourable ones in a misleading way.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>These are Google's rules, and breaking them can get reviews removed or a profile suspended. They are also UK law. Since April 2025 the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act has banned fake reviews and reviews that hide the fact they were paid for, and businesses are expected to take reasonable steps to make sure the reviews they publish are genuine.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Reply to every review</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Thank people for good reviews, briefly and by name. For a bad review, reply once, calmly: acknowledge the problem, say what you have done or will do, and invite them to contact you directly. Future customers read your replies as closely as the reviews themselves. Never argue, and never reveal private details about the customer.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">If a review is fake or abusive</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You can report a review to Google from your profile if it breaks their policies, for example if it is spam, offensive or clearly about a different business. Google will not remove a review just because it is negative.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-faq","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-faq"><!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How many reviews do I need?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There is no magic number. A steady flow of recent reviews matters more than a large total collected years ago.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Can I ask customers to mention my town or service?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It is better not to tell people what to write. Reviews in customers' own words are more convincing, and scripted ones can look fake.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Can I copy Google reviews on to my website?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You can quote them. Keep the wording exact, do not pick only the best in a misleading way, and make sure they are genuine.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Should I use Trustpilot as well?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Start with Google, because that is where local customers look. Add another platform only if your customers use it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
